ict what we  had is that the government invested huge  resources to strengthen the military  forces the US government supported that  process through monetary resources but  more importantly so through intelligence  and technical support so starting around  2006 the the government the national  government and the military forces were  able to exert Millett a major military  blows to the Girija so they were  isolated again in their traditional  strongholds and that I think said the  incentives for styling the peace talks  but we have been in peace talks for many  years in the 1990s the md80 maryam/mary  a group another Girija groups  immobilized in 2006 the paramilitary  groups to mobilize and now FARC follow  suit so we have been in a almost like a  third 30 year process of trying to  broker please okay  you mentioned internally displaced  people and it's a mind-boggling figure  15 percent of the population correct and  when you say internally displaced  internally they were displaced in other  areas of the country or they left the  country  no it's most is internal displacement is  within the geographical borders of  Colombia so the seven point four million  people does not include refugees but  mostly it was internally displaced  people going from rural areas of the  country to earth-2 they were urban areas  of the country because of the of the war  and the intense violence against the  civilian population I see okay and now  that peace has been brokered what will  what will happen to these people will  they be able to simply go back to their  home towns or  it doesn't seem to be that it would be  that easy since the word has been going  on for so long I think that most of  internally displaced persons will will  prefer to stay in there in urban areas  in some research that we have done  applying surveys to internally displaced  households they say they express that  they only 11 percent will be willing to  return to their to their hometowns I  expect that will change as violence  eases and as land starts being wasted to  the to the to the displaced population  but most of them will continue in the  urban areas what we have found in this  study is that for example female-headed  households are not willing to return  they prefer to stay in urban areas with  where health and education services are  larger and are of better quality we also  found that people that were heavily  traumatized before displacement are not  willing to return so mostly the people  who will return they are formal  landowners and agricultural workers but  let's say it's about 30% it would be  anyways 2 million people so it's going  to be a big challenge to to promote the  return of 30% of the population and the  government would have to provide support  for the return return process but also  to create income generation  opportunities to restitute land to  insert them again into the social  networks that they used to have in the  in their hometown so it's it's a big  challenge as well yes yes you had  mentioned the drug trade and I imagine  you mean cocaine specifically and that  had been such a big influence on the  conflict do you do you think that will  not rear its head again how will how  will Colombia keep that at bay cocaine  trade will continue ok even after the  conflict ends a cocaine trade is not a  cause of conflict but is basically it  has helped the country the conflict to  intensify and to amplify it throughout  the geographical territory cocaine train  is mostly  because it is illegal trade it has huge  profits for the people that participate  in illegal trade and it's also the cause  of a very illed designed policy  international policy to combat illegal  trade so that will continue after the  piece ends - - how illegal trade is  going - we really need an international  policy to change the the so-called war  on drugs has had immense cost for the  Colombian population a lot of people  have died and it has been vastly  ineffective so a study that was done by  Pasquale Restrepo and Sandra Russo -  Colombian economists show that to  eradicate one hectare of coca you have  to fumigate 30 hectares of coca so you  see how vastly ineffective it has been  so we hope that it will change but I'm  not very optimistic that that will  happen  mm-hmm do you foresee any change in the  the politics of the country in terms of  who is running the country that kind of  thing with the peace process moving  forward I don't anticipate that would  happen  hey FARC are not very popular they were  a very aggressive a guerrilla movement  they killed and victimized a very large  percentage of the population they might  be able to get elected in local  elections of the people of the places  where they were strong and that's very  isolated regions of the country but I  really don't anticipate that we will  have a shift for example to a leftist  movement in power in Colombia I think  there is a long way ahead one of the  consequences of the conflict in Colombia  the long conflict in Colombia is that  the population is very right-winged okay  so I think that's not very easy now an  election is coming up next week correct  in Colombia and the election is too what  will the election decide or a vote it's  always about the 2nd of October okay and  it's a plebiscite and the president was  not legally binded to call for a  plebiscite to support or not support  quartz but he decided he wanted to  submit the accords to the to the will of  the people so the purpose of the plan  aside is to say whether the population  is going to support or not the the peace  accord that was negotiated with with  FARC it's not a referendum it only has  only 13 percent of the registered to  vote population have to vote the  plebiscite in order to be effective but  it's an important and it's an important  process because a the president needs  the popular support sure sure and it  kind of legitimizes the what he's done  when do you anticipate any issues wi